  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 156 PM EST Wed Nov 25 2026\n\nModel trends have shifted over the last few days and the most recent \ndata favors snow across portions of our area on Thanksgiving Day \ninto Friday. This is due to the phasing of two shortwave systems, \none to our south and one to our north, and a resultant deep area of \nlow pressure tracking across the area over the next 48 hours. A few \nlight showers are expected through the rest of the afternoon before \nmore widespread showers move in overnight and continue into \nThanksgiving Day. During this time, an preexisting Wind Advisory \nwill remain in effect for the east TN mountains, southwest NC, and \nportions of southwest VA. Gusts in the mountains and foothills could \nreach up to 55 mph, potentially higher for the higher peaks such as \nCove Mountain, TN.\n\nTomorrow, Thanksgiving Day, rain continues through most of the day. \nThen, late afternoon into evening, colder air begins to spill into \nthe region on the backside of the low pressure system that will be \ntracking to our northeast. Rain will change over to snow for some \nareas as the cold air sweeps in from the northwest, with a rain/snow \ntransition and mix occurring overnight and into Friday. Some valley \nlocations, including the southern TN Valley, will even see some snow \nsnowflakes flying around. In terms of accumulation, no accumulation \nis expected across valley locations. Minor accumulations of up to \nan inch are possible across the higher elevations of the Cumberland \nPlateau by Friday morning. Minor accumulations from 1 to 2 inches \nare possible across the higher elevations of the east TN, southwest \nVA, and southwest NC mountains through Friday. HRRR, RRFS, REFS, NBM \nall seem to be in decent agreement on these forecast snow totals. \nFor the above areas mentioned to receive accumulation, a low \nprobability of minor travel impacts exists. As of now, no Winter \nWeather Advisory planned due to the low probability of only minor \nimpacts. \n\nThe bigger impacts will likely be due to a surge in winds \nThanksgiving evening into Friday due to a strengthening wind-field\nand tightening pressure gradient on the back side of the low. \nHRRR, REFS, and RRFS all show strong wind gusts beginning tomorrow\nafternoon/evening across a large portion of our area. These \nhigher wind gusts will continue overnight and through Friday \nafternoon. REFS means show areas along I-75 and eastward as being \nthe favored areas to see these stronger gusts, including valley \nlocations. REFS means generally show gusts between 30 and 40 mph. \nAlthough Wind Advisory criteria is for gusts of 40 mph and above,\nopted to issue a Wind Advisory due to the higher likely hood of \nimpacts due to Thanksgiving travel and family gatherings. Areas \nnot in the Wind Advisory are still expected to see gusts from 20 \nto 30 mph. \n\nPrecip begins to taper off on Friday but lingering precip is \nexpected across portion of northeast TN, southwest VA, and the east \nTN mountains through Friday night due to lingering moisture and \nnorthwest flow. \n\nWe clear out Saturday and zonal flow builds in behind the \ndeparted low. However, another system is fast approaching and will\nmove in on Sunday. Breezy conditions are once again expected \nacross our higher terrain areas due to a strengthening 850mb jet \nto around 40/50kts. A few thunderstorms are even possible as we \nwill be in the warm sector ahead of the front. Instability looks \nmarginal at this time so no severe weather threat currently \nanticipated but we will continue to monitor this system. \n\nAfter we clear out on Monday, the remainder of the week looks \npleasant with dry conditions and near seasonal temperatures.",
